W:2. And there once was a captain who sailed the salt seas
W:Sailed the salt seas all around-o
W:One day he espied this fair maid on the shore
W:How I wish that I had her on board
W:
W:3. Well the steward he ran and he lowered a boat
W:Quickly he rowed it to shore-O
W:The very first words that the steward did say
W:Fair maid, won't you venture on board?
W:
W:4. Through persuasion, cajoling, she finally agreed
W:And quickly he rowed her on board-o
W:The captain he smiled as he lifted her up
W:Fare thee well, sorrow and care
W:
W:5. Well the captain he poured out the rich, airy wine
W:It sparkled so clear and so bright-O
W:To see such a maid come aboard this ship
W:Fair maid I would welcome you now
W:
W:6. Well the captain he poured out the rich, airy wine
W:It sparkled so bright and so clear-o
W:Fair maid, you will spend all this night in my arms
W:And then I'll give you to my crew
W:
W:7. Oh thank you, oh thank you, this maiden replied
W:That's just what I was hoping for-o
W:For I've grown so weary of my maidenhead
W:As I walked all alone on the shore
W:
W:8. Well this maiden's lain down in the bow of the ship
W:She sang in a voice sweet and clear-o
W:She sang out the spell so enchantingly sweet
W:She sang captain and sailors to sleep (at her feet),
W:
W:9. Well she's robbed them of silver, she's robbed them of gold
W:She's robbed them of costly wear-o
W:And the captain's broadsword she has used as an oar
W:And she's rowed herself back to the shore
W:
W:10. And this maiden she stands all alone on the shore
W:Waving her handkerchief there-o!
W:For you are the captain that sails the salt sea
W:But I'm still a maid on the shore.
%
